Subject: JSON.parse reads a number in one pass and rounds 17-19 digit doubles with one long-double operation

What this does
  - `json_parse_number` collects the significant digits (up to 19) and the decimal exponent while it validates, instead of a second scan by `json_fast_double` (removed)
  - `json_wexp_to_double`: Clinger's fast path as before (w <= 2^53, |e| <= 22); then, on x86 with the 80-bit long double, w * 10^e or w / 10^-e for |e| <= 27 in long double and a rounding to double, unless the long-double result is exactly a midpoint between two doubles; everything else (more than 19 digits, larger exponents, midpoints) still goes to strtod
  - `<float.h>` included for `LDBL_MANT_DIG`

Why we need it
  Doubles printed by JS carry up to 17 significant digits, so most non-integral values in a payload (balances, coordinates, ratios) were above 2^53 and went to strtod, ~5% of parse cycles on the app-traffic benchmark, plus the second scan. json_app parse only: -6.9% cycles, -5.6% instructions.

Soundness
  w < 10^19 < 2^64 and 10^k for k <= 27 (< 2^64 * 2^27, with 27 trailing zero bits) are exact long doubles, so one multiply or divide gives r = the true value rounded once to 64 bits, round-to-nearest. A double midpoint needs 54 significant bits, so it is a long double; if one lay strictly between the true value and r it would be nearer the true value than r, a contradiction. So rounding r to double equals rounding the true value unless r is a midpoint (low 11 bits of the 64-bit significand exactly 0x400), which goes to strtod. The range stays normal for both formats (1 <= w < 10^19, 10^-27..10^46). Differentials identical old vs new: new jld.js (3,052,514 numbers: 14-20 digit decimals with exponents -35..35, leading and trailing zeros, exact double midpoints (2^53+2k+1)*2^j and *2^-q written several ways and their neighbours), jnum (400,015), jdtoa (1.5M), and all earlier JSON differentials.

